```

## Architecture

At a high level, Cpell follows the basic shell execution flow:

```text
User Input
    |
    v
Readline
    |
    v
Parser
    |
    v
Variable Expansion
    |
    v
Built-in Command?
   / \
 Yes  No
  |    |
  v    v
Execute  fork()
         |
         v
      Process Group
         |
         v
       execvp()
         |
         v
    External Command
```

For foreground processes, Cpell transfers terminal control to the child's process group and waits for it.

For background processes, Cpell keeps track of the process group as a job and immediately returns control to the user.

## Learning Goals

Cpell is primarily a systems programming and learning project.

The project is being developed to explore:

* Unix process creation
* `fork()`
* `execvp()`
* `waitpid()`
* Process groups
* Terminal process groups
* `tcsetpgrp()`
* Signals
* File descriptors
* `dup2()`
* File redirection
* Environment variables
* Unix job control
* GNU Readline
* C++ systems programming

## Roadmap

Planned improvements include:

* [ ] Pipelines (`cmd1 | cmd2`)
* [ ] More robust parser
* [ ] Quoted strings
* [ ] Escape characters
* [ ] Improved error handling
* [ ] More complete job control
* [ ] Proper `SIGCHLD` handling
* [ ] Job completion notifications
* [ ] Command aliases
* [ ] Additional shell built-ins
* [ ] Better POSIX compatibility
* [ ] Automated tests

## Why Cpell?

Most shells hide the underlying Unix mechanisms behind a simple command prompt.

Cpell is an attempt to build those mechanisms directly and understand what happens underneath:

```text
shell
  |
  +-- fork()
  |
  +-- process group
  |
  +-- terminal control
  |
  +-- signals
  |
  +-- file descriptors
  |
  +-- exec()
  |
  +-- waitpid()
```

The goal is not just to create another command-line interface, but to learn how a Unix shell actually interacts with the operating system.

